### What is a marker interface?

A marker interface has no abstract methods. It is completely empty but communicates a capability to the JVM or framework.

### What are the three classic examples?

```java
// Serializable: zero methods, marks class for byte stream conversion
public interface Serializable { }

public class Account implements java.io.Serializable {
    private String accountId;
    private java.math.BigDecimal balance;
}

// With Serializable: serialization works
java.io.ObjectOutputStream oos = new java.io.ObjectOutputStream(
    new java.io.FileOutputStream("account.dat"));
oos.writeObject(account); // works

// Without Serializable: NotSerializableException

// Cloneable: zero methods, marks class for clone()
public class Trade implements Cloneable {
    @Override
    public Trade clone() {
        try { return (Trade) super.clone(); }
        catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
    }
}
// Without Cloneable: CloneNotSupportedException
// Note: Cloneable is considered broken design. Prefer copy constructors.

// Remote: zero methods, marks class for RMI
public interface TradingService extends java.rmi.Remote {
    TradeResult executeTrade(String symbol, int qty) throws java.rmi.RemoteException;
}
```

### How does the JVM use marker interfaces internally?

```java
// Inside ObjectOutputStream (simplified):
public void writeObject(Object obj) throws java.io.IOException {
    if (!(obj instanceof java.io.Serializable)) {
        throw new java.io.NotSerializableException(obj.getClass().getName());
    }
    // proceed with serialization
}

// Inside Object.clone() (simplified):
protected Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
    if (!(this instanceof Cloneable)) {
        throw new CloneNotSupportedException();
    }
    // perform shallow copy
}
```

### How do marker interfaces compare to annotations?

| Aspect | Marker Interface | Annotation |
|--------|-----------------|------------|
| Methods | Zero | N/A |
| Type constraint | Yes: `<T extends Serializable>` | No: cannot use in generics |
| instanceof | Yes | No (use reflection) |
| Metadata | Cannot carry | Can: `@Cacheable(ttl = 60)` |
| Target | Classes only | Methods, fields, parameters |
| Hierarchy | Pollutes class hierarchy | Does not |

### How do you create a custom marker interface?

```java
// Custom marker: this account type supports international transfers
public interface InternationalTransferCapable { }

public class NRIAccount extends Account implements InternationalTransferCapable { }
public class SavingsAccount extends Account { } // no marker

public void transferInternational(Account account, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
    if (!(account instanceof InternationalTransferCapable)) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException(
            account.getClass().getSimpleName() + " does not support international transfers");
    }
    // proceed
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Modern designs prefer annotations for new tagging needs because annotations carry metadata and do not pollute the type hierarchy.
- Marker interfaces are still useful when you need a type constraint in generics: `<T extends Serializable>` cannot be done with annotations.
- In Spring, @Component is an annotation, not a marker interface, because it needs metadata like scope and name.
